1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,873
<font color="#FFFFFF">Graph neural networks are famously easy to fool
with small structural changes,</font>

2
00:00:04,953 --> 00:00:09,325
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the field has long treated that fragility as
pure adversarial vulnerability.</font>

3
00:00:09,405 --> 00:00:13,778
<font color="#FFFFFF">This ICLR 2023 paper from the Technical University
of Munich asks a sharper question:</font>

4
00:00:13,858 --> 00:00:18,231
<font color="#FFFFFF">do those small perturbations actually preserve a
node's true meaning?</font>

5
00:00:18,311 --> 00:00:22,684
<font color="#FFFFFF">Using a semantics-aware notion of an adversarial graph,</font>

6
00:00:22,764 --> 00:00:27,136
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors show that for low-degree nodes many
"small" edge edits already change the ground-truth label,</font>

7
00:00:27,216 --> 00:00:31,589
<font color="#FFFFFF">and that every graph neural network they test is
over-robust,</font>

8
00:00:31,669 --> 00:00:36,042
<font color="#FFFFFF">staying stubbornly unchanged even after the
semantics have flipped.</font>

9
00:00:36,122 --> 00:00:40,495
<font color="#FFFFFF">They then show that feeding the training graph's
label structure back in through label</font>

10
00:00:40,575 --> 00:00:44,947
<font color="#FFFFFF">propagation sharply cuts this over-robustness while
improving accuracy and genuine adversarial</font>

11
00:00:45,027 --> 00:00:49,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">robustness.</font>

12
00:00:49,780 --> 00:00:56,485
<font color="#FFFFFF">An adversarial example is supposed to be a small
change that does not alter the true category of the input.</font>

13
00:00:56,565 --> 00:00:59,402
<font color="#FFFFFF">For images a human can verify this by looking.</font>

14
00:00:59,482 --> 00:01:02,002
<font color="#FFFFFF">For graphs there is no such visual check,</font>

15
00:01:02,082 --> 00:01:06,567
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the community settled on counting edited edges
with an ℓ-zero budget.</font>

16
00:01:06,647 --> 00:01:09,738
<font color="#FFFFFF">But real graphs are dominated by low-degree nodes,</font>

17
00:01:09,818 --> 00:01:15,064
<font color="#FFFFFF">and for those nodes even a tiny edge budget can
completely rewire the neighbourhood.</font>

18
00:01:15,144 --> 00:01:20,961
<font color="#FFFFFF">That raises a fundamental and previously unanswered
question: do these standard perturbations</font>

19
00:01:21,041 --> 00:01:24,005
<font color="#FFFFFF">really keep a node's semantic content unchanged,</font>

20
00:01:24,085 --> 00:01:28,000
<font color="#FFFFFF">or are we attacking nodes whose true label has
already flipped?</font>

21
00:01:28,380 --> 00:01:33,748
<font color="#FFFFFF">The belief that graph neural networks are easily
fooled rests on the assumption that the</font>

22
00:01:33,828 --> 00:01:36,472
<font color="#FFFFFF">perturbations used are semantics-preserving.</font>

23
00:01:36,552 --> 00:01:39,319
<font color="#FFFFFF">Only a few works go beyond simple edge counts,</font>

24
00:01:39,399 --> 00:01:43,343
<font color="#FFFFFF">adding proxies like the degree distribution or
homophily metrics.</font>

25
00:01:43,423 --> 00:01:47,924
<font color="#FFFFFF">None of them directly measure whether the
ground-truth label is preserved.</font>

26
00:01:48,004 --> 00:01:53,372
<font color="#FFFFFF">This matters because if a "small" perturbation has
actually changed a node's true class,</font>

27
00:01:53,452 --> 00:01:58,015
<font color="#FFFFFF">then a model that keeps its prediction fixed is not
being robustly correct,</font>

28
00:01:58,095 --> 00:02:00,615
<font color="#FFFFFF">it is being wrong in a new and hidden way.</font>

29
00:02:00,695 --> 00:02:03,029
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors argue we need a principled,</font>

30
00:02:03,109 --> 00:02:07,920
<font color="#FFFFFF">label-aware notion of what a semantics-preserving
graph perturbation really is.</font>

31
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:11,756
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes four contributions.</font>

32
00:02:11,836 --> 00:02:15,292
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

33
00:02:15,372 --> 00:02:18,828
<font color="#FFFFFF">it defines a semantics-aware notion of adversarial
robustness for node-level predictions,</font>

34
00:02:18,908 --> 00:02:22,364
<font color="#FFFFFF">and with it a brand new concept for the graph
domain called over-robustness,</font>

35
00:02:22,444 --> 00:02:25,900
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is robustness against admissible
perturbations whose ground-truth label has already changed.</font>

36
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:29,436
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

37
00:02:29,516 --> 00:02:32,972
<font color="#FFFFFF">using Contextual Stochastic Block Models it shows
that common perturbation sets contain</font>

38
00:02:33,052 --> 00:02:36,508
<font color="#FFFFFF">a large fraction of graphs with changed semantics
and that every examined GNN is significantly</font>

39
00:02:36,588 --> 00:02:40,044
<font color="#FFFFFF">over-robust,</font>

40
00:02:40,124 --> 00:02:43,580
<font color="#FFFFFF">with matching patterns on real-world graphs.</font>

41
00:02:43,660 --> 00:02:47,116
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

42
00:02:47,196 --> 00:02:50,652
<font color="#FFFFFF">it shows that folding the known label structure
into inference through label propagation</font>

43
00:02:50,732 --> 00:02:54,188
<font color="#FFFFFF">significantly reduces over-robustness with no cost
to accuracy or adversarial robustness.</font>

44
00:02:54,268 --> 00:02:57,724
<font color="#FFFFFF">Fourth,</font>

45
00:02:57,804 --> 00:03:01,260
<font color="#FFFFFF">it proves that classifying an inductively sampled
node carries no robustness-accuracy tradeoff.</font>

46
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:07,063
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key idea is to introduce a trusted reference
classifier that knows the true semantics.</font>

47
00:03:07,143 --> 00:03:12,567
<font color="#FFFFFF">On Contextual Stochastic Block Models the authors
can derive the Bayes optimal classifier,</font>

48
00:03:12,647 --> 00:03:15,686
<font color="#FFFFFF">which chooses the most likely class given the data,</font>

49
00:03:15,766 --> 00:03:18,315
<font color="#FFFFFF">and use it as the ground-truth reference g.</font>

50
00:03:18,395 --> 00:03:24,919
<font color="#FFFFFF">An over-robust example is then a perturbed graph
where the model and the reference agree on the clean input,</font>

51
00:03:24,999 --> 00:03:29,444
<font color="#FFFFFF">the perturbation flips the reference (so the
semantics genuinely changed),</font>

52
00:03:29,524 --> 00:03:32,502
<font color="#FFFFFF">but the model stubbornly keeps its old prediction.</font>

53
00:03:32,582 --> 00:03:39,167
<font color="#FFFFFF">They measure over-robustness as one minus the ratio
of semantics-aware robustness to conventional robustness,</font>

54
00:03:39,247 --> 00:03:45,588
<font color="#FFFFFF">so a value of zero-point-two means twenty percent
of the measured robustness lies beyond semantic change.</font>

55
00:03:45,668 --> 00:03:49,685
<font color="#FFFFFF">Finally, they combine graph neural networks with
label propagation,</font>

56
00:03:49,765 --> 00:03:56,350
<font color="#FFFFFF">feeding the training graph's known labels into the
inference process to reduce this unwanted over-robustness.</font>

57
00:03:56,730 --> 00:04:01,081
<font color="#FFFFFF">The controlled experiments run on Contextual
Stochastic Block Models,</font>

58
00:04:01,161 --> 00:04:05,319
<font color="#FFFFFF">which let the authors compute the Bayes optimal
reference exactly.</font>

59
00:04:05,399 --> 00:04:09,685
<font color="#FFFFFF">Each graph has one thousand nodes and is
parametrized to mimic CORA,</font>

60
00:04:09,765 --> 00:04:14,115
<font color="#FFFFFF">matching its expected number of same-class and
different-class edges.</font>

61
00:04:14,195 --> 00:04:18,225
<font color="#FFFFFF">A parameter K controls how discriminative the node
features are,</font>

62
00:04:18,305 --> 00:04:19,894
<font color="#FFFFFF">swept from zero-point-one,</font>

63
00:04:19,974 --> 00:04:24,839
<font color="#FFFFFF">where features carry almost no signal and structure
matters most, up to five,</font>

64
00:04:24,919 --> 00:04:27,150
<font color="#FFFFFF">where structure becomes unnecessary.</font>

65
00:04:27,230 --> 00:04:32,544
<font color="#FFFFFF">They inductively sample a thousand test nodes per
graph and average over ten graphs.</font>

66
00:04:32,624 --> 00:04:39,800
<font color="#FFFFFF">Results are corroborated on the real-world Cora-ML
graph and on a Barabási-Albert model with community structure.</font>

67
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:41,799
<font color="#FFFFFF">The findings are striking.</font>

68
00:04:41,879 --> 00:04:43,367
<font color="#FFFFFF">For a majority of nodes,</font>

69
00:04:43,447 --> 00:04:49,051
<font color="#FFFFFF">the standard perturbation sets are full of graphs
whose true label has already changed.</font>

70
00:04:49,131 --> 00:04:53,559
<font color="#FFFFFF">For example, at K equal to one and a per-node
degree-plus-two budget,</font>

71
00:04:53,639 --> 00:04:59,635
<font color="#FFFFFF">ninety-nine-point-four percent of target nodes have
a perturbed graph with changed semantics.</font>

72
00:04:59,715 --> 00:05:03,555
<font color="#FFFFFF">And every graph neural network tested shows
over-robustness.</font>

73
00:05:03,635 --> 00:05:09,108
<font color="#FFFFFF">A perfectly robust reference like an MLP exhibits
forty-three percent over-robustness</font>

74
00:05:09,188 --> 00:05:12,636
<font color="#FFFFFF">at K equal to zero-point-one under the weak ℓ₂ attack,</font>

75
00:05:12,716 --> 00:05:18,517
<font color="#FFFFFF">meaning forty-three percent of its measured
adversarial robustness is actually undesirable</font>

76
00:05:18,597 --> 00:05:20,738
<font color="#FFFFFF">robustness beyond semantic change,</font>

77
00:05:20,818 --> 00:05:24,070
<font color="#FFFFFF">and all the GNNs cluster close to that upper bound.</font>

78
00:05:24,450 --> 00:05:28,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">The most informative ablation concerns label propagation.</font>

79
00:05:28,290 --> 00:05:32,050
<font color="#FFFFFF">Applying it on top of a graph neural network
sharply lowers over-robustness.</font>

80
00:05:32,130 --> 00:05:35,890
<font color="#FFFFFF">For instance,</font>

81
00:05:35,970 --> 00:05:39,730
<font color="#FFFFFF">a GCN combined with label propagation at K equal to
zero-point-five drops its over-robustness</font>

82
00:05:39,810 --> 00:05:43,570
<font color="#FFFFFF">to about twenty-one percent,</font>

83
00:05:43,650 --> 00:05:47,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">and label propagation on its own achieves the
lowest over-robustness of any method.</font>

84
00:05:47,490 --> 00:05:51,250
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially,</font>

85
00:05:51,330 --> 00:05:55,090
<font color="#FFFFFF">this comes for free: adding label propagation does
not hurt test accuracy and often improves</font>

86
00:05:55,170 --> 00:05:58,930
<font color="#FFFFFF">genuine adversarial robustness while structure
still matters.</font>

87
00:05:59,010 --> 00:06:02,770
<font color="#FFFFFF">The effect is robust to stronger attacks too,</font>

88
00:06:02,850 --> 00:06:06,610
<font color="#FFFFFF">though some over-robustness always remains,</font>

89
00:06:06,690 --> 00:06:10,450
<font color="#FFFFFF">such as eleven-point-four percent for a GCN under
Nettack and nineteen-point-two percent for an MLP.</font>

90
00:06:10,830 --> 00:06:12,989
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers summarize the impact.</font>

91
00:06:13,069 --> 00:06:18,681
<font color="#FFFFFF">Up to ninety-nine-point-four percent of target
nodes have perturbations that change their</font>

92
00:06:18,761 --> 00:06:21,175
<font color="#FFFFFF">true label under a common threat model.</font>

93
00:06:21,255 --> 00:06:27,251
<font color="#FFFFFF">A perfectly robust classifier shows forty-three
percent over-robustness at low signal strength.</font>

94
00:06:27,331 --> 00:06:32,176
<font color="#FFFFFF">Label propagation cuts a GCN's over-robustness to
roughly twenty-one percent,</font>

95
00:06:32,256 --> 00:06:37,293
<font color="#FFFFFF">and even under the strong Nettack attack
over-robustness never fully disappears,</font>

96
00:06:37,373 --> 00:06:41,450
<font color="#FFFFFF">sitting around eleven to nineteen percent depending
on the model.</font>

97
00:06:41,830 --> 00:06:46,225
<font color="#FFFFFF">The one-line takeaway is that graph neural networks
are not simply fragile,</font>

98
00:06:46,305 --> 00:06:51,355
<font color="#FFFFFF">they are over-robust: a large part of their
measured robustness is stubborn robustness</font>

99
00:06:51,435 --> 00:06:55,174
<font color="#FFFFFF">that persists after the node's true meaning has
already changed,</font>

100
00:06:55,254 --> 00:06:58,992
<font color="#FFFFFF">which conventional evaluations wrongly credit as
good behaviour.</font>

101
00:06:59,072 --> 00:07:04,123
<font color="#FFFFFF">Bringing the training graph's label structure into
inference through label propagation</font>

102
00:07:04,203 --> 00:07:09,253
<font color="#FFFFFF">reduces this over-robustness while improving
accuracy and real adversarial robustness.</font>

103
00:07:09,333 --> 00:07:11,521
<font color="#FFFFFF">And with a semantics-aware definition,</font>

104
00:07:11,601 --> 00:07:16,174
<font color="#FFFFFF">classifying a newly added node carries no
robustness-accuracy tradeoff at all,</font>

105
00:07:16,254 --> 00:07:20,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">changing how robustness in graph machine learning
ought to be measured.</font>
